<v Michelle>Hey, good.

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:25.010
<v Orlando>Didn't know I could sing the <lang pt-BR>lobo mau</lang>, did you?

00:11:26.270 --> 00:11:32.590
<v Orlando>But there's also the word, the
adverb, "poorly", like <lang pt-BR>ele canta mal</lang>.

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:34.470
<v Orlando>And those sound the very same, right?

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:36.000
<v Orlando>So say for me "the bad wolf".

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:38.050
<v Michelle><lang pt-BR>Lobo mau.</lang>

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:40.050
<v Orlando>and he sings poorly.

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:40.940
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Ele canta mal.</lang>

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:42.160
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Mal</lang>, <lang pt-BR>mau</lang>.

00:11:42.380 --> 00:11:45.250
<v Orlando>Even though one's spelled with an L, one's
spelled with a U, it sounds the very same.

00:11:46.100 --> 00:11:48.000
<v Orlando>Where in Spanish it would be totally different, you see:

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:52.000
<v José Luis><lang es>Malo</lang> and <lang es>mal</lang>.

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:56.210
<v Orlando>Anyway, so that's the idea of the final L.

00:11:56.260 --> 00:11:57.800
<v Orlando>I think we've covered the subject today, haven't we?

00:11:57.850 --> 00:11:58.800
<v Michelle>Yeah.

00:11:58.850 --> 00:12:04.823
<v Orlando>Why don't we hear the dialogue one last time and we'll let you
do it at more like a native speed so they can hear it, but again,

00:12:04.823 --> 00:12:07.250
<v Orlando>we're focusing on the sound of the syllable-final L.

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:16.050
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>No Brasil a gente sempre tem alguém que revela as</lang>
<lang pt-BR>nossas fotos. E aqui, qual é o procedimento ideal?</lang>

00:12:16.100 --> 00:12:22.300
<v Michelle><lang pt-BR>Aqui não falta lugar que tenha essas máquinas de</lang>
<lang pt-BR>alta tecnologia para imprimir fotos multicoloridas.</lang>

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:28.250
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>A última vez que revelei as minhas a cor azul</lang>
<lang pt-BR>ficou muito saltada. Não gostei do resultado final.</lang>

00:12:28.850 --> 00:12:34.263
<v Michelle><lang pt-BR>Eh, aqui quem revela as fotos somos nós mesmos.</lang>
<lang pt-BR>É só salvar num CD ou levar o cartão de</lang>

00:12:34.263 --> 00:12:39.550
<v Michelle><lang pt-BR>memória da máquina, selecionar no painel</lang>
<lang pt-BR>as fotos que você quer e imprimir no papel.</lang>

00:12:40.350 --> 00:12:47.050
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Nada mal, hein? Culturas diferentes, né? Mas é fácil</lang>
<lang pt-BR>usar essas máquinas? É igual a um caixa eletrônico?</lang>

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:53.300
<v Michelle><lang pt-BR>É super fácil. Você pode revelar mais</lang>
<lang pt-BR>de mil fotos e o custo não é alto.</lang>

00:12:53.350 --> 00:12:56.436
<v Orlando>Excellent, thanks. As always, check us out online at <lang pt-BR>Tá Falado</lang>.

00:12:56.436 --> 00:13:01.922
<v Orlando>You can download the PDF file to see all this written
down, and you can also go into the blog and make comments.

00:13:01.922 --> 00:13:06.330
<v Orlando>And by the way, we do thank those who do write
comments into the blog. They're fun to see.

00:13:06.830 --> 00:13:09.550
<v Orlando>And with that, we're at the end of Lesson 21, <lang pt-BR>Tá Falado</lang>.

00:13:09.850 --> 00:13:11.800
<v José Luis><lang es>Hasta luego.</lang>

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:12.800
<v Valdo>See you next time.

00:13:12.850 --> 00:13:15.850
<v Michelle>Bye.
